.TH HIRZ\-UHID\-BROKER 8 2026-11-16 hirz "System Administration"
.SH NAME
hirz\-uhid\-broker \- root helper that creates the hirz virtual security key devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hirz\-uhid\-broker
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-uhid
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-min\-uid
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-connections
.IR N ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hirz\-uhid\-broker
owns
.I /dev/uhid
and creates one FIDO HID device for each client that connects to its Unix socket.
The client is
.BR hirzd (1),
running as the user.
.PP
For each connection the broker reads the peer credentials (SO_PEERCRED), refuses uids below
.BR \-\-min\-uid ,
a uid that already has a device, and connections beyond
.BR \-\-max\-connections .
It then creates the device with fixed values (name "hirz virtual authenticator", bus USB,
the client's uid in the phys field and the FIDO report descriptor) and relays 64\-byte
reports in both directions.
Nothing in the device description comes from the client.
When the client disconnects, the device is destroyed.
The uhid descriptor never leaves the broker, and descriptors sent by clients are never
accepted.
The broker logs uids, pids and report counts, never report contents.
.PP
Normally the broker is started by systemd socket activation through
.B hirz\-uhid\-broker.socket
(socket
.IR /run/hirz/uhid.sock ,
mode 0666; any local user may connect and the broker enforces the uid policy).
After setting up the socket it drops every capability and sets PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS,
PR_SET_DUMPABLE 0 and RLIMIT_CORE 0.
As uid 0 it opens the root\-owned
.I /dev/uhid
through the owner permission bits, so it needs no capability.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " PATH"
Bind
.I PATH
itself (mode 0666, parent folder created with mode 0755) instead of using a socket passed
by systemd.
For manual runs.
.TP
.BI \-\-uhid " PATH"
The uhid device (default
.IR /dev/uhid ).
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-uid " N"
Refuse peers with a uid below
.I N
(default 1000).
0 is rejected.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-connections " N"
Devices at the same time, one per uid (default 8, at least 1).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage and exit with status 2.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 2
on wrong arguments,
.B 1
when the socket cannot be set up, the process cannot drop its capabilities or harden itself,
or
.I /dev/uhid
cannot be opened.
Otherwise the broker runs until it is stopped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/hirz/uhid.sock
The socket.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hirz\-uhid\-broker.{socket,service}
The systemd units.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/{70,72}\-hirz.rules
udev rules: each hidraw node of a hirz device is owned by the uid that created it (mode
0600) and gets no seat ACL.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/modules\-load.d/hirz.conf
Loads the uhid kernel module.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run by hand for a test, with
.B hirzd
started by the user in another terminal:
.PP
.nf
.RS
sudo hirz\-uhid\-broker \-\-listen /run/hirz/uhid.sock
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hirzd (1),
.BR hirz (1),
.BR systemd.socket (5)
.PP
.I docs/uhid.md
in the hirz sources.
